course: everyday
lesson: 431
title: When the story falls apart
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Mia told Mum she'd been at the library. «What book did you get?» «A book about… sharks.» «Let's see it.» «I had to leave it there.» «Why?» «Because… sharks.» Four questions and the story fell over. Mia had really been at the shopping centre with kids she'd been told to keep away from. She was grounded, and the next Saturday Mum went to the library with her — a bit embarrassing, but kind of okay.
explain: A made-up story has no real memories behind it, so questions knock it over; the truth stands up to any question. Tiny clues help too: Grace said the crisp packet under her bed was «from ages ago», but the best-before date was next year and Mum had bought those crisps on Tuesday. Zoe said she'd walked straight home — then her brother said «you were at the shop», and the shopkeeper waved: «Hiya Zoe, back for more?» Zoe laughed and owned up. Jasmine blamed the last cookie on her sister, then the dog, then «maybe it just fell». Dad: «Three suspects for one cookie. Very impressive.»
explain: Some lies use things that are too big to play with. Ethan in Dallas invented a big kid who stole his lunch money — he'd spent it on Pokémon cards — and his parents were scared for his safety for nothing. Oisin in Cork said his dog had died to get more time; the teacher sent a sympathy card home, and Mum opened it with the dog asleep on her feet. «You used my kindness,» said the teacher, and Oisin felt like an eejit (Irish for a fool) for a month. Kirsty in Inverness said her book was «at Dad's»; her parents live apart, and when the teacher asked Dad, he hadn't seen her for a month — now the teacher was worried about something else entirely.
explain: Bodies don't work like excuses either. Rohan in Singapore was too sick for the quiz, fine by lunch to play, and sick again when homework came out. «Can or not?» asked Mum — Singapore talk for «yes or no?» — and he did the quiz at home. Arlo told the class he broke his arm skateboarding; he'd slipped in the bath. When the cast came off he told his best mate, who laughed for ages and then taught him to skate for real.
explain: Ben's teacher drew a «lie ladder»: one lie on the bottom rung, and every rung needs another lie to hold it up. «When it gets too high, it falls — and you fall from the top.» Ben drew seven rungs and wrote underneath: «I'd have stopped at one.»
fact: A made-up story has no real memories behind it, so simple questions make it fall apart.
fact: The truth stays the same however many questions are asked.
fact: Small clues, like dates on packets, witnesses and shopkeepers who know you, often break a lie.
fact: When a story changes every time it is told, people stop believing all of it.
fact: Inventing someone who hurt you is a big lie: it scares people who love you and could get an innocent person in trouble.
fact: Lying that someone has died or is ill, to get out of things, is cruel to the people who believe you.
fact: Using family matters, like parents living apart, as cover can make grown-ups worry and touches real feelings.
fact: Real illness doesn't come and go to match homework and play, so fake sickness shows a pattern.
fact: A funny true story, like slipping in the bath, is usually a better story than a cool lie.
fact: The «lie ladder» picture shows that each lie needs another to hold it up, and the higher it gets the worse the fall.
fact: The earlier you say «actually, that wasn't true», the easier it is.
fact: «Eejit» (Ireland) means a fool; «can or not?» (Singapore English) means «yes or no?»
exercise: How many questions did it take for Mia's library story to fall apart? | About four | One hundred | None, Mum believed her | Just one, she gave up at once
exercise: Where had Mia really been? | At the shopping centre with kids she'd been told to avoid | At the library reading books about sharks and whales | At her gran's house for tea | At the swimming pool with her cousin and her cousin's friends
exercise: What did Ethan really do with his lunch money? | Spent it on Pokémon cards | Lost it on the bus | Gave it to a big kid | Saved it in a jar
exercise: What hurt Ethan's parents most? | He made them scared for his safety for nothing | He spent all his money on cards | He missed his lunch and was hungry all afternoon | He told the school before telling them
exercise: What clue broke Grace's «from ages ago» crisp story? | The best-before date and when Mum bought them | Crumbs all over her pillow and her duvet | The dog sniffing under the bed | Her big brother's word against hers
exercise: How was Oisin's dead-dog story found out? | Mum opened the teacher's card with the dog on her feet | The dog barked loudly during a phone call with the teacher | The teacher visited the house | The vet rang the school to say the dog was well
exercise: What did Oisin's teacher say? | «You used my kindness» | «That's a lovely dog you've got» | «You can have more time» | «Never mind, it's fine»
exercise: Who gave Zoe away after she said she walked straight home? | Her little brother and the shopkeeper | Her teacher and the lollipop lady | A camera at the school gate | Nobody, she got away with it in the end
exercise: What really happened to Arlo's arm? | He slipped in the bath | He crashed his skateboard | He fell off a bike | He hurt it at football
exercise: What did Arlo's best mate do when he heard the truth? | Laughed for ages, then taught him to skate | Told the whole class before the bell went | Stopped being his friend | Said he'd lied about his own broken arm too
exercise: Jasmine blamed the cookie on her sister, then the dog. What did she say next? | «Maybe it just fell» | «It was Dad» | «It was me» | «There never was a cookie»
exercise: What pattern did Rohan's mum notice? | Sick for school, fine for games | Sick every single Monday morning | Sick only at Grandma's | Fine for school, sick for games
exercise: Why did Kirsty's lie make the teacher worry? | Dad hadn't seen her for a month, so the teacher feared something else | The book was very expensive and the school wanted it back | Dad lives very far away | The teacher thought Dad had taken the book and kept it for himself
exercise: What is the «lie ladder»? | A picture where each lie needs another to hold it up | A ladder kept in the teacher's room for high shelves | A climbing game the class plays at break | A list of punishments
exercise: What did Ben write under his seven-rung ladder? | «I'd have stopped at one» | «I'd climb right to the top» | «Ladders are dangerous» | «Seven is my lucky number»
exercise: Why can the truth stand up to any question? | It is backed by real memories | It is always told loudly and clearly | Nobody ever asks it any hard questions | It changes to fit each question
test: Dad: «Three suspects for one cookie. Very impressive.» Is he really impressed? | No, he's joking because the story keeps changing | Yes, he loves a good story with lots of suspects | Yes, he thinks the dog did it | No, he's furious and shouting at all three
test: Mum in Singapore: «Sick or not? Can or not?» What is she asking Rohan? | To pick one — is he sick or fine? | Whether he can open a can | Whether he wants a drink | Whether he can do the quiz in a can
test: Mate: «just say your dog died, they'll give you loads of time». Why is that a cruel lie? | It uses people's kindness about something sad | Teachers don't like dogs very much | It won't get any extra time | It's not cruel, it's a clever trick that hurts nobody
test: Oisin felt like an eejit for a month. What's an eejit? | A fool | A hero | A teacher | A dog
test: «a big kid nicked my money!!» — but there was no big kid. What could have happened to an innocent kid? | He could have been blamed and in trouble | Nothing, because made-up kids can't be blamed | He would get the money back | He would get a prize for being honest
test: Zoe's shopkeeper waves: «Back for more?» What does Zoe's laugh and confession show? | Real life has many witnesses, so the truth is easier | Shopkeepers always tell on kids | She should use a different shop next time, further away | Lying works better in big towns
test: What would happen if you were asked the same question about something true ten times? | The answer would stay the same | The answer would change each time | You'd forget the truth | You'd have to make up details
test: Arlo: «skating sounds cool, bath sounds dumb». What's the lesson? | A funny truth makes a better story than a cool lie | Always pick the cooler story, people like it more | Never tell anyone you fell | Bath stories should be kept secret
test: Kirsty's teacher rang Dad, and now the grown-ups are worried. What should Kirsty do? | Explain everything and say sorry to both parents | Say Dad got the dates wrong | Stay quiet and let it all pass | Blame the teacher for ringing Dad without asking her first
test: In a cartoon, a kid's lie gets bigger and bigger until it is a giant monster chasing him. What does that show about real lies? | Each lie needs more lies, and it gets out of control | Lies can turn into real monsters | Small lies always stay small, only big ones grow | Running away from a lie makes it go away
test: Grace: «that packet's been there ages, I swear». Mum reads the date out like a detective. What gave Grace away? | A tiny clue — the date and the shopping day | A camera hidden in her room | Her sister telling on her at dinner | A crumb trail leading all the way to the kitchen
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a fabricated account frequently fails under questioning. | It is not supported by genuine recollection | Questions are prohibited in respect of fabrications | Fabricated accounts are always shorter than true accounts | Listeners are invariably informed of the fabrication in advance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a false report that a family pet has died. | It exploits the sympathy of those deceived and causes them hurt when discovered | It is a harmless means of obtaining additional time for the completion of work | It is permissible where no pet is owned | It causes no distress to the teacher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a false accusation that another child has committed theft? | It alarms the parents and may bring an innocent child into trouble | It is harmless where no child is named | It improves the safety of the school | It is an acceptable explanation for money lost in the course of the school day
exam: It is given that a pupil's illness appears solely when schoolwork is due and disappears when recreation is available. Determine the most probable inference. | The illness is feigned | The illness is genuine and serious | The illness is caused by the school building | The pupil recovers rapidly from any illness
